            "full-response": "**Answer:** B: no\n\n**Reasoning:**  \nC-kit (CD117) is a receptor tyrosine kinase involved in cell proliferation and survival. While it is overexpressed in certain cancers (e.g., gastrointestinal stromal tumors), its expression in HBV-induced conditions (chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, HCC) is not a standard diagnostic marker. HBV-related hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) often exhibits mutations in tumor suppressor genes (e.g., p53, TP53) rather than c-kit. Additionally, c-kit is not typically associated with HBV pathogenesis. Since there is no established diagnostic role for c-kit in these contexts, the answer is **no**. \n\n**Answer:** B: no",
